.TH FIND 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
find \- search for files in a directory tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B find
walks the directory tree under each
.I path
and prints, one a line, every path that matches all the tests in the
.IR expression ,
the starting point included.
With no
.IR path ,
it starts at
.BR . ;
with no tests, it prints everything.
.PP
The paths are the arguments before the first one that starts with
.B \-
or is
.BR ! .
Symbolic links are never followed (as GNU
.BR find 's
.BR \-P ):
a link is listed as a link, and not walked into.
.SH EXPRESSION
All the tests must hold, as if joined by
.BR \-a .
.TP
.BI \-name " pattern"
The last part of the path matches the shell
.I pattern
(\fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB[...]\fR, as
.BR sh (1)
globs).
.TP
.BI \-iname " pattern"
Like
.BR \-name ,
ignoring case.
.TP
.BI \-path " pattern"
The whole path, as printed, matches the shell
.IR pattern ;
here
.B *
matches
.B /
too.
.TP
.BI \-type " c"
The file is of type
.IR c :
.B f
a regular file,
.B d
a directory,
.B l
a symbolic link.
.TP
.BR \-not ", " !
Turns the next test around.
.TP
.BI \-maxdepth " n"
Descend at most
.I n
levels below the starting points (0: the starting points only).
It applies wherever it appears.
.TP
.BI \-mindepth " n"
Print nothing less than
.I n
levels below the starting points.
.TP
.B \-print
Print the path; what
.B find
does anyway.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a path could not be read, or for a bad expression.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
find /lib \-name "*.lua"
find . \-type d \-maxdepth 2
find /home ! \-iname "*.txt"
.fi
.SH NOTES
There is no
.BR \-o ,
no parentheses, and no actions besides
.B \-print
(no
.B \-exec
or
.BR \-delete ).
A starting point cannot begin with
.BR \- .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR tree (1),
.BR grep (1)
